\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. Let $P\in\mathbb C[z]$ have degree $n\ge2$ and zeros $z_1,\ldots,z_n\in\overline{\mathbb D}$, listed with multiplicity. A critical point is a zero of the derivative $P'$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every zero $z_j$ of $P$, must there be a critical point $w$ with $|w-z_j|\le1$? Equivalently,
\[\forall j\in\{1,\ldots,n\}\ \exists w\in\mathbb C:\ P'(w)=0,\quad|w-z_j|\le1.
\]
The quantifier includes every chosen zero, not only a zero of maximum modulus. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does every zero of a polynomial whose zeros lie in the closed unit disc have a critical point within distance one?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2304005 (AMR-022-4005). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory}, arXiv:1809.07200, 2018. Problems 4.5 and 4.30 (the latter explicitly defines the degree-at-least-two class), including complete Update 4.5. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
